 more work needs to be done  the the kind of the broad color-coded  goal here over time of having more  health insurance is to turn more of  these light blues and whatever basically  the lighter the very light blue and the  and the medium blue to turn it black  because black here is good black here  means  lower level of out-of-pocket expenditure  the inverse is higher levels of health  insurance coverage this was the other  slide I think that Arielle showed it's a  little counterintuitive here because we  get the same countries in black both but  in both senses but in both of them but  here this is the inverse these are the  countries where at least the ILO  according to its data is saying that  there is a high degree in the darker  shaded countries a higher degree of  coverage and I would have thought  where's the Rwanda delegation here over  there I would have said I know Rwanda is  a small country right up there but it  should be black I think because you were  just telling me that everyone and I have  some slides actually later on just one  slide showing the coverage is very very  high now in Rwanda through its health  insurance system and I think Ghana also  through the NH is what about 60% of the  population I believe is covered now and  there's an ambition to go much higher so  I think this map is already out of date  there are two outstanding African  countries and I think Mexico that's  probably fair Mexico has a high degree  of coverage especially through through  Social Security so this is the central  thesis that I'm putting out there that  we can debate it's it's not without its  controversy but this is the strategic  the grand strategic opportunity that I'm  putting out there for you to consider  which is that health insurance risk  pooling of one kind or another and there  are many different variants on how to do  health insurance and risk pooling that  it drives a whole series of changes  throughout the the the that's the system  that have an impact on health status and  that have an impact on the rest of the  dynamics of the health system that is  maybe a bit of a bias of somebody like  myself who's an economist but I think  that there's a lot of evidence that  money here and the way in which funds  are collected the kind of protection  that's and risk risk sharing that's  that's driven or that's afforded through  health insurance it actually has a whole  series of concatenating or repercussions  throughout the health system some of the  I think many of them very positive some  of them perhaps that could be a negative  and that need to be managed but that  it's a very powerful very powerful  instrument for driving change in health  systems and whether you want to look at  the impact that it has on the financial  protection and the health of the  population or the catalytic changes in  the systems itself that it's a very very  powerful driver and one which needs to  be very carefully designed carefully  managed in order to achieve the positive  impacts that it can bring and in order  to mitigate or avoid some of the  downsides and their downsides to every  every policy change whatever might be  its upsides but reducing the  out-of-pocket expenditures and this risk  of catastrophic expenditure which drives  families into bankruptcy or into poverty  is certainly one of the very powerful  dimensions of this using health  insurance in a very carefully designed  way to increase utilization of health  services and hopefully health services  that really make a difference to health  outcomes I think again over lunch in  with the Rwanda colleagues there was a  lot of mention I believe you mentioned  that you you noticed in your own  practice that with the advent of health  insurance or the expansion that the  numbers of people coming in and  demanding services had gone up to fold  or to it you said from 40 patients you  gave the example of 50 we would show up  now and you attributed a lot of that to  the financial security that came out of  health insurance of course the next  question is what are they coming in for  what types of health issues are they  coming for not that all of them are not  valid health issues but in terms of some  of Rwanda is basic health goals around  reducing maternal mortality or child  child illness and child mortality  infectious disease are people coming in  and demanding and receiving good quality  services to deal with those health  problems that the country sees as being  central in terms of the catalytic  changes in health systems we can talk  about that during the discussion period  but it because of the way in which  insurance allows for a separation of  provision and an  financing or purchasing and creating  what what I call here a platform for  strategic purchasing there are a whole  series of things that can be done in  terms of trying to achieve national  health goals it can give patients more  of a choice of their provider it can  bring in private NGO and other non-state  providers in a way that wouldn't be  possible otherwise it can be targeted  toward essential services that have a  large health gain behind them certainly  can be used also to try to promote the  equity objectives that governments see  as part of their responsibility to try  to preferentially serve the needs of  those who are most disadvantaged it can  certainly incentivize quality and we  talked about that a bit over lunch the  Rwanda example may not be easy to  translate into Mexico there may be  special reasons why it can't just be  carried and plopped down in in one of  the one of the states in Mexico but  there may be some ideas there that you  can grasp that can be useful and  certainly in to the extent that as I say  here money follows the patient  expressing both the the demand or the  need of the patient the opportunity or  the the the stimulus to make providers  accountable to patients for what they do  in terms of timeliness and quality and  other things like that as well as  outcomes insurance may be a very  important part of the answer in terms of  the changes and the choices this is a  very stylized  look at how health financing and health  insurance evolves over time I think  there was another version of this shown  by arielle yesterday but this is an  adapted version of it if you compare a  number of the countries here today which  have modest incomes per capita say 500  to $1,000 per capita which i think is  the case of many of the african  countries here a relatively small share  of GDP or of national wealth going into  health around 4 to 6 percent typically  so this these boxes ought to be smaller  in volume than the ones on the right  because as Ari  say yesterday as countries economies  grow the share of national wealth which  goes to health tends to rise over time  the sort of iron law that he showed with  those graphs yesterday but typically  it's the out-of-pocket expenditure that  predominates the government is a large  financier and then you tend to have  smaller amounts of either social health  insurance where the I think this is the  Mexico experience covering about half of  the population where payroll taxes are  used both employee and employer taxes  are put into a pool and cover those who  actually contribute maybe not all  population but you have this as an  important way of pooling risk for a part  of the population it tends not to  necessarily be a universal system in  most developing countries with a few  exceptions like Colombia and and yeah I  guess Colombia is the one that I would  say right now and then you have the  community health insurance using local  mechanisms for collecting and often  retaining at local level premiums  sometimes subsidized by the government  in order to again pool risks but  typically across a smaller population  group but where this would be for most  of you here what are the choices you  face going forward typically as you move  toward health insurance and risk pooling  the share of out-of-pocket expenditure  goes down dramatically private health  insurance may come in in a in a modest  or a larger way but in most OECD  countries in most developed countries  private insurance tends to be small and  supplementary to the larger national  systems which tend to be either on the  national health service model along the  lines of what was developed in Britain  after the Second World War I believe  that one of the speakers yesterday  referred to beverage and the national  health system and then there's the  tradition of the social health insurance  models which much of Latin America has  picked up over the last century but I  think in many ways the historical  antecedents there come out of Germany  out of the Bismarck in period at the end  of the nineteenth century and that model  based on way wage taxes has been picked  up in an  number of the European countries such as  the Netherlands and a few others and  these pentas tends to be a choice rather  than a both it's an either-or and that's  been historically the pattern that  doesn't mean that the countries that you  come from may develop some different  system or some hybrid but those are  typically the models that countries have  moved toward over the last century  century an hour and a half and as I said  up here a much larger share of national  wealth which is also growing fight by by  definition going into the health sector  so the choices facing low-income  countries and I would say middle-income  countries like Mexico Mexico's typically  classified by the International Monetary  Fund and the World Bank as a  middle-income very solidly middle-income  country with per capita income perhaps  five to ten times what it is in many of  the other countries here today but still  I would say in general the the questions  tend to break down into these three  areas first of all are we ready for  social health insurance is there the  political momentum because this is not  just a technical decision this is very  much a political decision I was hearing  again from Rwanda I'm sorry if I keep  mentioning Rwanda but there are some  historical circumstances some of them  not very pleasant in the in the  country's history over a decade or more  ago that perhaps the the the the  positive side of that is that it's led  to a very strong political consensus and  political will to deal with some of  these issues like health insurance so  each country faces a different set of  historical and political antecedents but  there has to be at least a conversation  and a decision at a high level to move  toward this because it's not just a  health problem this is a this is an  issue that concerns the whole ministries  of Finance within government tend to and  ought to pay a lot of attention to what  happens in health insurance i husband  have a big impact on employment if you  use wage taxes it has many many effects  and of course it has a very large impact  on the whole medical care sector and  there are a lot of interest vested  interests that may or may not want to  see the kinds of changes toward health  insurance not everyone  will necessarily benefit from the  creation of a national health insurance  of one kind or another secondly what  kind of system to you or do we want to  pursue in the long run and it tends to  break down into three key areas where is  the money going to come from what's the  source of revenues is it going to be  general taxes these wage taxes local  premiums paid out of pocket at least to  finance a part of it  what's the mix where's the money going  to come from and how is it going to be  pooled together secondly how many  purchasers and what type of purchasers  are we going to have in the German  system it's a decentralized multi payer  system it's not a single government it's  not the German government collects these  wage taxes and pays all providers it's  it's a it's a decentralized sub  nationalized system with a number of  different purchasers they may compete  they may not compete so that's another  big issue and then the third one is who  in this kind of system is eligible to  provide the health services is is this  going to be a publicly delivered system  a mixed system predominantly private a  lot depends on the starting point for  each country and again these political  choices and as I said earlier at least  to stylize the choices that you face  looking at what's happened historically  not that you have to be a prisoner of of  history here by any means but there are  the national health system or national  health service type approaches using  tending to use general non earmarked  taxes or revenues a largely public  provision that's the UK system then of  course the social health insurance model  that I mentioned before I referred to to  Germany but it's also the system that  Korea very consciously copied about 25  years ago and now has a very in some  ways a very successful health insurance  system in many ways it was the  inspiration for what's happened in  Colombia over the last 15 years as the  country perhaps in Latin America that's  gone the furthest with social health  insurance and I say half of Mexico  because I believe through the two big  social security institutions in Mexico  about half that population is covered  but still there's this anguish concern  what about the other 50% what what kind  of service do they get and is it fair  are they getting good quality services  how do we improve the the protection and  the health services for the other 50%  and then finally you have some private  insurance options and and the US and and  Brazil to a certain extent Switzerland  to a certain extent use certain forms of  private health insurance it also has a  certain number of advantages but it also  has a number of disadvantages certainly  the ability to achieve the kind of  universal coverage that's implicit in  what many countries here want to do  would suggest that the approaches under  private health insurance I have some  very serious drawbacks so the other big  question first you have to ask the  question where do you where do you want  to end up what kind of a health  insurance systems you want to have ten  years 20 years from now I don't know if  that's the most of your problem solving  was one two three years so and that may  be the the political lifetime of many of  the policy makers that you know in your  countries and that we know in ours  moving to a to a large health insurance  system is almost certainly going to take  more than three years so maybe in terms  of the methodology we're thought  yesterday it's also important to think  in terms of stages and and some  intermediate milestones along the way  not that we can ever predict exactly how  these things are going to evolve so  adaptation and adjustment are also  important but the other question is how  do we get from where we are today to  where we need to go with health  insurance and I think that the most  important sort of lesson that's been  learned there is that you can end up in  almost any number of places from  whatever your starting point is today if  you know where you want to go but it  makes sense to try to build on what is  on what is out there already because  where there are community health  insurance schemes like in like in Rwanda  or social health insurance schemes as in  other countries it's easiest to build on  them then there's the question of the  the scope and the pace with which you  move and there are options you can go  slowly and  and coverage very gradually that may  risk some type of political reversal you  can also go for the big bang and try to  change things very rapidly that can be  messy but it also gets you there faster  and then you can sort out the other  issues that arise under those  circumstances there's also the question  of the affordability of what you want to  guarantee as the benefits package that  you're going to cover and this is where  a number of countries have really run  afoul and I'm not just talking about  low-income countries I'm thinking for  example of the Czech Republic after the  liberalisation there in the early 1990s  when a very generous health insurance  package which was paid to doctors on a  fee-for-service basis very quickly  bankrupt at the Czech health insurance  system which was paid out of wage taxes  so the size of the benefits package and  how you pay for it are very very  important and then there's the issue of  whether this is going to be done largely  with domestic resources or whether  there'll be a mix of domestic and  external support in terms of some of the  stumbling blocks I mentioned them  earlier so I won't go through them in  any great detail but again that's the  trilogy that I would suggest you think  about revenues the benefits package and  the issue of the payment system that  underlies it because each one of those  can either make a health insurance  system sink or swim and as there there  there are there there are many ways that  health insurance system can come  crumbling down many many ways it's not  easy and it's it's worth being vigilant  and thinking through all of these risks  as you go forward in terms of solutions  again there isn't time to go through  them all we can pick them up in the  discussion but whether it's in the  revenue area the benefits package or  payment systems there are solutions  which have been developed and  implemented and have worked well in  other countries so my suggestion without  going through each one of these is your  country is unique it can't necessarily  assume that what happens somewhere else  will be applicable but it's worth  learning the lessons of what other  countries are doing and in that sense  again it's great that Ghana and  Rwanda are here if there were more time  Betsy we could almost have an entire  week just talking about how to reform  health financing and and getting the  different countries to talk to one  another it would probably be a lot more  worthwhile than listening to me  blabber on about what I know because the  each country here telling each other  about its experience could probably  maximize the learning and the ability to  formulate responses um  I say that there's not if this is not a  blank slate and I think it's important  that among the low income countries that  have really advanced dramatically in  this area two of them are here in the  room really two of the outstanding  examples that we in results for  development when we've documented  innovations in health finance we  constantly come back to Robert to Ghana  and Rwanda so I feel very honored that  I'm here this week with those two  countries because they really are the  pioneers Ghana with its national health  insurance system Rwanda building up on  the mutual the small local local locally  sort of autonomous systems which have  been brought together in and woven  together in a national system how they  started small and got big perhaps they  can tell us about in the discussion but  there are also other countries that are  not here Nigeria which has some very  interesting experiments right now using  HMOs health maintenance organizations  India which is a itself a vast  laboratory for experimentation in in  health finance it's unbelievable it's  like a continent or or or a planet unto  itself when it comes to things that are  going on right now in the health  insurance area we can't we can't keep  track of them all we've been trying to  monitor them but there are a huge number  of things going on there and I did want  to mention although Mexico is a  middle-income country I put it here at  the end because Mexico under its recent  health minister Julio Frank has made a  very bold move to create a kind of  insurance for the 50 percent who are  still not covered by Social Security  whether it's the perfect solution I know  that from talking to the Mexican  delegation they're struggling with how  to improve the use of this people's  insurance that's what the gurukul are  means in Spanish it's not the the story  is  at its end it's perhaps at its middle or  its beginning but I know this is  something you're working very hard on  this was the Ghana system I'm not going  to run through this but Ghana collects  the money up here at the top through a  number of different channels including  wage taxes and value-added tax so it's a  mix of general taxation or or at least  taxation on consumption across the board  and individual wage taxes it also has  small user fees and it also draws in  premiums that are collected through  other other sources and then it it uses  it to allow patients to go to a mix of  public and private health facilities in  the case of Rwanda I said I would at  least show one picture it looks like I  need to add another another dot  somewhere up here because you were  saying now that Rwanda is up to 95  percent or 97 percent coverage but this  is this is almost unprecedented if it's  true if it can be verified and if this  is really leading to the kind of social  protection or financial protection that  health insurance can give this is quite  a remarkable expansion of the insurance  in Rwanda in terms of some of the  lessons learned just to finish up these  are broad generalizations each one can  be questioned and challenged I would do  the same but certainly what you start  with the voluntary and the  community-based financing systems can  help to build confidence in the larger  prepaid schemes as people get used to to  participating and to contributing  something I think it's two dollars per  person in Rwanda it's not a huge amount  of money although for some households  that might be considerable this is a way  to build public interest and support the  key thing is to just to pool full risks  whether it's through the social health  insurance approach or the national  health system approach may be of less  importance than the political decision  to move to this type of universal  coverage and to go at it in a systematic  problem-solving way because problems  will come up along the along the journey  no matter how well you plan ahead of  time as I said earlier my three little  make sure the benefits package the  revenue collection and the payment are  very carefully examined and and managed  because that's where these systems tend  to rise or fall the political commitment  that's that's very clear but also the  management and administrative capacity  to support these systems that's what  many of you here are so good at  evaluated each stage and keep adapting  and don't forget that overall as Arielle  said yesterday if the country's economy  is not growing it's very hard to put any  type of a health insurance system in  place I know you're not the ministers of  finance or economy but it does matter so  this is where I'm finishing just with  some questions that we could take up in  the discussion for Ghana and Rwanda that  have already traveled far with this  maybe you could talk a little bit about  the most difficult problems we know  you've done a lot of good things but  what are the big problems you face can  you can you can you can you share with  us what what worries you about the  progress you've made and whether there  may be some backsliding and whether  there's some new issues which have come  up and how are you dealing with those  for countries like Ethiopia and Liberia  that are here what are your plans in the  health insurance area where is the  country going with this and what are the  big questions and worries you have or  perhaps the others can can be helpful  and then I say here this is this was  written I think before I had lunch today  and I wrote down are there lessons from  the reform of the social health  insurance system and the saguru popular  in Mexico that could be useful for the  African countries what I heard over  lunch was the reverse  I heard Mexico mostly listening to